  "query_preview": "..\n\nTonight: An area of low pressure responsible for the cloud cover \nand showers across southeastern WA and Idaho Panhandle today \nwill finally drift south of the area with clear skies returning \nto the entire Inland NW. Winds will be light and generally 5 mph\nor less. Additionally, Sunday's winds ushered in a dry, \ncontinental air mass with dewpoints currently in the 20s. This \ncombination sets up the Inland NW for strong radiational cooling\nand cold temperatures heading into Tuesday morning. A hard \nfreeze is expected across the mountain valleys of NE WA and N \nID. For the lowlands, temperatures will vary from the mid 20s to\nlower 30s. Those zones still in climatological growing season \nwill have the potential for their first freeze. This includes \nthe Okanogan Valley, Wenatchee Area, Moses Lake Area, L-C \nValley, and Lower Garfield and Asotin Counties. There is a 70% \nchance for temperatures to fall below freezing for the Moses \nLake Area. For the Okanogan Valley, L-C Valley, and Lower \nGarfield and Asotin Counties, there is a bit more uncertainty \nwith exact readings. Forecast leaned toward the 10th percentile \nNBM forecast given the aforementioned synoptic conditions. This \nresulted in temperatures closer to 33-34F on the benches but \n29-33F for the lower elevations, especially for many of the \nsmaller communities outside of Omak, Lewiston, and Pomeroy \nwithin their respective zones. The one area that came up with \nlittle to no freezing temperatures was the Wenatchee Area. \nProbabilities were near 0% and even the 10th percentile \nforecasts were 35F or warmer. Will need to see how the high \ntemperatures checkout this afternoon and decide if this area \ncould be poorly modeled and need to be added. \n\nTuesday-Friday: A quiet weather pattern is on tap for the the \nInland NW this week. A ridge of high pressure will be over the \narea Tuesday, briefly flatten some Wednesday, then amplify again\nfor Thursday and Friday. Outside some passing clouds on \nWednesday, skies will largely remain clear. Winds will be light \nand terrain driven. Temperatures will warmer than normal with \nafternoon highs climbing into the 60s through Thursday then \n60-70s Friday. Nights will be cool dropping into the 30s. Going \nto be a great week to get outdoor projects wrapped up, recreate \nin the outdoors, or just enjoy a glass of lemonade under October\nsunshine. \n\nSaturday-Monday: Medium range models continue to advertise a \nbreakdown of the ridge. The initial blow comes through around \nSaturday (80% ensemble support) producing gusty westerly winds \nand little in the way of precipitation. This paves the way for a\ndeeper trough to move into the Northwest Sunday into Monday \n(80% ensemble support) bringing a better chance for showers and \ncooler than normal temperatures. At this time, we are not \nanticipating a widespread soaking precipitation event but some \nareas could pick up over a quarter of an inch of QPF. Snow \nlevels come down at this time falling closer to 5000-7000 feet. \n/sb",
